WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:04.850
We have reached the domain of troubleshooting
and error handling.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:10.400
In this section on error handling, we will
explore three types of errors common to Python

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.400
code.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.880
As we go through these examples, I hope that
you will develop an eye for spotting problems

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:21.890
in code before they happen or at least know
where to look for errors once they do happen,

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:26.269
as even the best programmers spend about a
third of the time coding and two-thirds of

00:00:26.269 --> 00:00:28.660
the time making code work.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.500
The first group of errors to cover is the
group of syntax errors.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.570
Syntax errors in code are like punctuation
and spelling errors in writing.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:40.130
The difference between the two is that in
programming, syntax errors cause code not

00:00:40.130 --> 00:00:46.039
to run, whereas in writing, people might misunderstand
what you write or form conclusions about you

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:47.039
as a writer.

00:00:47.039 --> 00:00:52.240
In this 511-syntax file, I have three syntax
errors.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.980
Can you spot them?

00:00:54.980 --> 00:01:00.070
Unlike some errors, which occur during the
execution of the program, syntax errors prevent

00:01:00.070 --> 00:01:03.570
the code from even being compiled and executed
in the first place.

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:09.091
Python's compiler checks for syntax correctness
before running any code, and a syntax error

00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:11.390
will halt this process.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:15.400
One common syntax error is forgetting the
colon at the end of a block definition, which

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:22.159
is easy to do kfor more experienced programmers
learning Python because most programming languages

00:01:22.159 --> 00:01:24.770
use curly brackets to define those blocks.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:31.150
In contrast, Python uses a colon and then
indentation on the lines part of a block.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:36.140
We are missing a colon at the end of this
function definition on line 2, so I will add

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:38.290
that colon.

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:44.310
Speaking of indentation, we stressed elsewhere
in this course the importance of keeping spacing

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:47.430
consistent with indents.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.240
Are the indents the same on lines 3 and 4?

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.240
No.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.430
That is a syntax error.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:59.070
I will adjust the indent on line 3 to fix
that syntax error.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.050
Do you see the third syntax error anywhere?

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:05.289
Look at line 6.

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:10.530
We have a mismatch on the open and closed
parentheses.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:16.280
Another common syntax error involves opening
something, like parentheses or quotation marks,

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:19.250
and not closing them properly.

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:25.580
I will add a closing parenthesis to the end
of line 6, and at this point, the syntax errors

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.080
should be fixed.

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.270
I will run the code.

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:40.190
Syntactically, the code works, but the print
statement on line 4 did not print.

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:45.730
Though we are covering logic errors in the
next topic, what we see on line 4 is an example

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:47.599
of a logic error.

00:02:47.599 --> 00:02:53.310
Lines 3 and 4 are out of order, as, in a function,
once a return statement is coded into the

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:57.950
function, that function is done.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:14.330
I will reverse the code on those two lines,
rerun the code, and now the code works as

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:15.190
intended.

